component SigilCompilationEventProtocol {
  goal {
    Own compatible streaming of long-running compiler progress and authoritative
    terminal outcomes.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      CompilationEvent is a versioned JSON Lines envelope containing protocolVersion,
      runId, sequence, type, and payload

      a completed terminal event contains the authoritative CompilationReport
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationEventProtocol {
  constraints {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      JSON Lines mode writes only CompilationEvent envelopes to standard output.
      Human progress and host logs use standard error.

      Sequence values increase within one run and one terminal event ends the stream.
      Failed and cancelled runs emit terminal events whenever reporting remains
      possible.

      A completed method settlement returns the same authoritative CompilationReport
      carried by the completed terminal event. Cancellation and host or protocol
      failure return no CompilationReport and reject with COMPILER_CANCELLED or
      COMPILER_FAILED respectively, whether or not transport remains writable long
      enough to emit the corresponding terminal event.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      Decision: Use a versioned JSON Lines stream with one authoritative report in
      the completed terminal event.

      Scope: Governs compiler streaming to every host surface.

      Trade-offs: Consumers process an ordered stream instead of one JSON object.

      Discarded alternatives: Mixed stdout and unframed JSON were rejected because
      they cannot safely carry incremental events.

      Consequences: Hosts validate compatibility, ordering, completion, failure, and
      cancellation consistently.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      A successful run emits ordered progress then one completed event containing the
      report.

      Cancelled and failed runs emit their corresponding terminal event when the
      process can still write it.

      Cancellation after work begins rejects with COMPILER_CANCELLED and emits one
      cancelled terminal event when transport remains writable.

      A host or protocol failure rejects with COMPILER_FAILED and emits one failed
      terminal event when transport remains writable.

      Loss of writable transport may prevent a terminal event but does not change the
      method rejection identity.
    }
  }
}
